A||||6||ChadMan||DVD questions||||5||
Z||000000||ChadMan||10-11-2000||10:04 AM||||I'm thinking of replacing my 12-year-old home CD player (nickname: Ol' Skippy) with a DVD player.  Does anybody here have a DVD player that's not part of their PC?  How do you like it?  Any particular features I should look for when shopping for one?<P>Also, is it possible to tape store-bought and rented DVD movies on VHS, or are the DVD's scrambled to prevent this?  (And please, no lectures about copyright violations - if I rent "E.T." on DVD and make a VHS copy for my shut-in grandmother, I think Steven Spielberg will still be able to afford to pay his electric bill.)<P>Thanks in advance!||||reg||5
Z||000001||Gold-86SE||10-11-2000||10:17 AM||||<BR>I have a sony DVD player for my entertainment system.  It is a single CD/DVD player and I purchased it this year.  I like the performance and the quality of the picture.  However, my particular player does not recognize burned music CDs - I know I have tried.<P>As for options look for DTS output - this is an audio coding format, and S-video outs - this is a digital cable that carries both audio and video signals - it substitutes for the RCA connectors.  If you are using an HDTV then S-video is probably very important for the clarity of signal, if you are using a regular TV, RCA connectors are just fine.  Depending on your entertainment set-up you will want to have multiple audio channel outs.<P>Personally, I prefer separate components for my entertainment system: Receiver, DVD/CD player, etc.  The reason I say this is because some stores sell a Receiver/DVD combo this is good if you don't care about upgrading, I, however, do care about upgrading, so I purchase separate components so as the components go out-of-date I can replace a single component and not a mess of stuff.<P>DVDs can be had for as little as 150 bucks, maybe cheaper, but you get what you pay for. <P>That's my 2 cents.<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.txconnect.com/home/rharris/signature-a.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||10-11-2000||02:22 PM||||I have a Toshiba that is about a year old. I would get the DTS and S-video outputs. As for transferring them to VHS tapes, You need to set the audio output to 2-channel and use the RCA outs. The DTS or the upper level surround sound channels will transfer to the VHS tape but the VCR's cannot decode them. Your sound quality will drop. Just record it in 2-channel stereo and it will be like a regular Video tape.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||10-11-2000||02:43 PM||||Orief,<BR>Good info. Have you done this? I was under the impression that DVDs could not be copied onto VHS tape at all. Some sort of encoding or whatnot that caused the VCR to not be able to sync the signal. That's the major reason I haven't bought one, yet. That and money.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR>88 Formula T-Top <BR>88 Coupe (soon to receive transplant.)<BR>88 "pretzeled" Formula <P>Out of my mind.<BR>Back in five minutes.<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 10-11-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000004||Oreif||10-11-2000||09:40 PM||||I did it for the newer american Godzilla movie so my son (big Godzilla fan) could watch it in his room (his VCR) instead of using the TV (with DVD player) in the family room. (he has watched the movie at least 30 times!) I had to record it twice, first time I checked it out and it sounded like it was deep under water. After I figured out why the sound was messed up I had to record it again. <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 10-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Standard||10-12-2000||01:57 AM||||I just got a sony DVD a few months ago, the DVP-S560D.  Mine won't read CD-R's either, kinda pisses me off, but I still need to buy a seperate CD player.  I've been trying to stick with Pioneer equipment put I'm very impressed with the quality of the Sony stuff.  It has 5.1ch output, digtal out coax, digital out optical, 2 video outs, 2 S video outs, and a component video out.  Dolby Digital, DTC, Digital Cinema Sound.. Lots of cool stuff  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Lots of stuff to play around with in the on-screen menu.  I love technology!  Got it for $250 at Best Buy, open item with nothing missing  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||burdell1||10-13-2000||01:12 AM||||Don't hesitate..BUY A DVD PLAYER!!! if you love movies (like I do) then it is a must have...and this is from someone who used to think that DVD players would never be accepted by the public. Boy was I wrong...I have a Philips 940AT and it is great..it was around $250...The picture is flawless...I've never tried recording it onto VHS tapes, but I've always heard that you couldn't.....<P>This one does play back CD-Rs and VCDs...<P>If you want a good test of a DVD player get the movie "A Bug's Life" because that movie was recorded digital to digital so the picture is as perfect as it is gonna get....<BR>If you have any questions you can e-mail me<P><BR>||||reg||1
